[Messages]
Coalition Representative: We’ve just been notified that the committee hearing on the plastic bag fee ordinance is locked in for two weeks from now. The clerk circulated the draft ordinance, last month’s staff memo, and a couple of studies from other cities.

Coalition Representative: Our members are extremely worried. They see this as yet another hit to already thin margins, and there’s a lot of anger about how this could play out at the checkout counter. We need to go in strong with Councilmember Garcia and Garcia’s policy aide so they understand why this proposal is unacceptable from the perspective of local retailers.

Coalition Representative: Please draft a sharp, forceful memo—no more than two pages—that we can send directly to Garcia’s office. It should:
- Clearly identify that it is prepared on behalf of the Local Retailers Coalition;
- Describe how the fee would be applied at checkout in a way that underscores the practical headaches and costs for retailers;
- Hammer home the likely negative impacts on small and medium-sized shops—extra administrative work, point-of-sale changes, tense interactions with customers over a new fee, and unfair competition with larger players who can absorb the disruption more easily;
- Only briefly acknowledge the stated environmental goals, mainly to show that this ordinance is a blunt and unfair tool that puts too much of the burden on retailers;
- Push hard for the outcomes we want, whether that is stopping the ordinance as drafted, significantly weakening or narrowing it (for example, broad exemptions for small businesses), or shifting to alternative approaches that don’t put new fees at the checkout counter.

Coalition Representative: We understand you need to stay within legal and ethical expectations, but within that space we want this memo to be a strong piece of advocacy for our side—not a balanced overview. The only audience will be Councilmember Garcia and Garcia’s policy aide. Let me know if you need any further detail from our members before you get started.
